/*
RMSTEST_24 - default player ring radius and variance, with no circle_radius
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 1, Sec.6.1's player land placement

QUESTION
Two questions, and the second one matters more.

First, the constants. With no circle_radius attribute at all, Sec.6.1 uses
radius 40 [tune] and variance 20 [tune], from guide:845 "the standard radius
for unconstrained lands is around 40" and guide:849 "20 seems to be close to
the standard amount of variance when not using circle_radius". Both are the
guide author's estimates of an engine default, hedged in the guide's own words.
Neither has been measured.

Second, and this is the one that changes the rendering, WHAT IS 40 A
PERCENTAGE OF. Sec.6.1 says circle_radius values are a percentage of map width
and that the default follows the same rule, but "percentage of map width"
admits two readings that differ by a factor of two.
  40 percent of the full width   ->  48 tiles from center on a 120 map
  40 percent of the half width   ->  24 tiles from center on a 120 map
On a 120 map the half width is 60, so the first reading puts the player ring at
48 of a possible 60 and nearly on the map edge, and the second puts it halfway
in. Those are completely different maps. The preview draws whichever we pick
and there is currently no evidence for either.

METHOD
Eight player lands on a SNOW base with no circle_radius anywhere, each land
small enough to stay well separated. Each player land is one 4-connected GRASS
patch, so the eight centroids are the eight origins. Compute each origin's
distance from map center and read the mean for the radius and the spread for
the variance.

Base terrain is SNOW rather than WATER on purpose. An island layout on water
would be the natural way to separate player lands, and it would also make DE
auto-paint a BEACH ring at every land and water boundary, which shifts the
patch edges and is the exact trap that made the first attempt at item 7c
unreadable. No water, no beach, no correction to reason about.

GENERATE AT TINY (120) WITH 8 PLAYERS. Small map so the two candidate radii,
48 and 24 tiles, are far apart relative to the variance.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. Eight GRASS patches, one per player. Fewer means two lands merged and the
   centroids between them are not origins.
2. Mean distance from center lands near 48 rather than near 24, because the
   guide's phrase is "percentage of map width" without qualification. Stated as
   a prediction precisely because it is the reading Sec.6.1 already assumes and
   therefore the one at risk of being believed without checking.
3. The eight origins are evenly spaced in angle with a random whole-ring
   rotation, so the angular gaps are near 45 degrees each while the absolute
   angles differ from run to run.
4. The eight radii are NOT identical. Variance 20 predicts a visible spread. If
   all eight come back at the same distance, the implicit jitter does not exist
   and Sec.6.1's "no circle_radius" branch should use variance 0, which would
   also mean guide:849's estimate is describing something else.

RUN IT FIVE TIMES. Eight origins is a small sample for a variance, and the ring
rotation differs per run, so five runs gives 40 radii, which is enough to
separate a real spread from a fixed ring.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches GRASS
  Map center on a 120 map is 59.5, 59.5. Distance from center is
  sqrt((cx-59.5)^2 + (cy-59.5)^2) on the printed centroids.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain SNOW

  /* No circle_radius, deliberately. The absence IS the test. */
  create_player_lands
  {
    terrain_type GRASS
    number_of_tiles 60
    base_size 3
    clumping_factor 20
    border_fuzziness 0
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
